  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Saturday) \nIssued at 1034 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nWeak upper-level ridging and surface high pressure build back across\nthe Southern Plains on Monday, bringing sunny skies and rebounding\nafternoon temperatures into the upper 50s to lower 60s. Quiet and\npleasant fall weather will continue Tuesday and Wednesday as southerly\nreturn flow re-establishes, pushing daytime highs into the low to mid\n60s.\n\nLow-level moisture return will increase mid-to-late week. A \nquick-moving shortwave trough will move across the \nCentral/Northern Plains Wednesday into Thursday. Surface ridging \nover the Appalachians may draw in enough low-level moisture for \nlow-end precipitation chances (10-20%) Wednesday night into \nThursday morning, mainly across eastern Oklahoma, as the shortwave\ntrough departs. Otherwise, most of Thursday and Friday look dry \nand mild, with high temperatures reaching the upper 60s and lower \n70s. Ensemble suites and long-range deterministic models indicate \nanother and more potent upper-level trough ejecting off the\nRockies and into the Plains Friday night into Saturday. While \nthere remains a big spread in model solutions regarding exact \nevolution, timing, and position of the approaching trough, low-to-\nmedium rain chances (20-50%) were introduced ahead of an \nassociated cold front forecast to move through Saturday \nafternoon/evening. Details will continue to be fine-tuned over the\nnext several days. Temperatures should cool back toward seasonal \naverages behind the front by next Saturday.\n\nMejia",
